WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:10.308
It only takes a few seconds for one of the most beautiful and peaceful experiences nature has to

2
00:00:10.408 --> 00:00:12.108
offer to turn into a disaster.

3
00:00:12.828 --> 00:00:21.271
In November of 1998, four experienced young Canadians were cross-country skiing through the spectacular Rocky Mountains in Kokanee

4
00:00:21.331 --> 00:00:22.952
Glacier Park, British Columbia.

5
00:00:23.836 --> 00:00:29.181
The next moment, the group found itself plummeting down a steep slope, tossed about by one of the

6
00:00:29.221 --> 00:00:31.883
most powerful forces in nature: an avalanche.

7
00:00:32.684 --> 00:00:36.508
Three members of the group survived the dangerous wall of flowing ice and snow.

8
00:00:37.589 --> 00:00:44.535
But one, Michel Trudeau, the 23-year-old son of Pierre Trudeau, a former prime minister of Canada, was swept

9
00:00:44.595 --> 00:00:46.537
into the icy depths of Lake Kokanee.

10
00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:50.180
Unfortunately, no one could reach him in time and he drowned.

11
00:00:51.264 --> 00:00:57.888
Trudeau's killer was an enormous mass of snow mingled with ice, soil, pebbles, and possibly boulders that rushed

12
00:00:57.948 --> 00:00:58.908
down the mountainside.

13
00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:05.812
Known in some regions as a "snowslide," an avalanche is almost always a terrible, destructive event, one that

14
00:01:05.872 --> 00:01:08.433
few plants and animals have the ability to survive.

15
00:01:09.374 --> 00:01:14.076
Fortunately, avalanches are not everyday occurrences in most mountain regions.

16
00:01:15.097 --> 00:01:22.817
They require four specific conditions before the snow can start sliding: a steep slope, deep snow cover consisting

17
00:01:22.837 --> 00:01:27.079
of several distinct layers including at least one weak layer, and a trigger.

18
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:33.342
They are, therefore, most common in snowy, mountainous regions, such as the Alps or the Himalayas.

19
00:01:34.623 --> 00:01:40.666
An avalanche occurs when the force of gravity, which is always trying to pull snow downhill, exceeds the

20
00:01:40.726 --> 00:01:44.508
force between the molecules of ice that keep snowflakes stuck together.

21
00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:49.271
The layers of snow that build up over time, however, aren't all the same.

22
00:01:50.352 --> 00:01:57.146
For example, layers of snow laid down during extremely cold temperatures will be different from those deposited during

23
00:01:57.166 --> 00:01:57.787
warm spells.

24
00:01:58.847 --> 00:02:04.289
The layers will have different moisture levels, and the amount of moisture in the snow affects how well

25
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.610
snowflakes bind together.

26
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.973
If a lower layer is composed of ice crystals that don't stick together well, the layers of snow

27
00:02:12.073 --> 00:02:15.654
on top can suddenly break free and begin coasting downhill.

28
00:02:16.475 --> 00:02:19.256
A common trigger is often nothing more than a loud noise.

29
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.723
When the conditions are just right, thunder or even a loud shout can be enough to set the

30
00:02:24.763 --> 00:02:26.104
deposit of snow in motion.

31
00:02:27.125 --> 00:02:34.210
For this reason, mountain guides often demand that hikers keep absolutely silent when crossing areas where avalanches are

32
00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:40.895
common. Ski resort operators, however, use the ability of noise to set off an avalanche to their advantage.

33
00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:49.248
To avoid dangerous accumulations of snow, they trigger small avalanches with explosives, which create shock waves.

34
00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:54.450
These shock waves shake the weak layers of a snowpack enough to break the bonds between the layers

35
00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.611
of snow and initiate a snowslide.

36
00:02:57.551 --> 00:03:01.712
This way, there is a lower chance that a hiker or skier could trigger one and die as

37
00:03:01.732 --> 00:03:02.212
a result.

38
00:03:03.093 --> 00:03:06.794
Predicting the timing of a natural avalanche is almost impossible.

39
00:03:07.714 --> 00:03:12.616
But experienced mountain guides and snow experts can often tell where one is likely to happen.

40
00:03:13.468 --> 00:03:19.374
Nearly all avalanches, as much as 90 percent, occur on mountain slopes of 30 to 45 degrees.

41
00:03:20.235 --> 00:03:27.642
Almost none happen on slopes of less than 25 degreesi—t's too flat—or on inclines steeper than 50 degrees,

42
00:03:27.722 --> 00:03:29.724
where snow never gets a chance to build up.

43
00:03:31.245 --> 00:03:37.451
Avalanches most commonly begin above the tree line, where there are no trees to help hold the snowpack

44
00:03:37.511 --> 00:03:37.972
in place.

45
00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:45.351
Once an avalanche gets started, however, it can continue down a mountainside well below the tree line, through

46
00:03:45.411 --> 00:03:47.352
narrow gullies and along cleared roads.

47
00:03:48.432 --> 00:03:53.854
Like anything that falls toward the ground, the farther an avalanche travels, the more speed it picks up.

48
00:03:54.735 --> 00:03:57.416
And the faster it travels, the more force it acquires.

49
00:03:58.356 --> 00:04:04.059
The largest avalanches move so fast that they can produce a furious wind while the snow tumbles down

50
00:04:04.119 --> 00:04:07.220
a mountain, steamrolling almost everything in their paths.

51
00:04:08.368 --> 00:04:12.392
Even thick stands of trees are no match for the power of a sizable avalanche.

52
00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:18.457
Entire villages have found themselves buried under several meters of snow in a matter of minutes.

53
00:04:19.438 --> 00:04:26.324
Happily, few avalanches take place in areas that are properly maintained, like ski resorts and popular hiking trails.

54
00:04:27.325 --> 00:04:33.170
Nevertheless, people are buried alive every year because they were in the wrong place at the wrong time,

55
00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.072
and did not take precautions.

56
00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:42.059
According to the Colorado Avalanche Information Center, the way to avoid getting caught in an avalanche is to

57
00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:43.400
avoid dangerous slopes.

58
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:51.044
If venturing into remote mountains in the winter, you should carry an inexpensive, handheld tool called an inclinometer.

59
00:04:51.904 --> 00:04:56.386
Smaller than a paperback book, an inclinometer measures the angle of a mountainside.

60
00:04:57.227 --> 00:04:59.508
This simple piece of equipment could save your life.

61
00:05:00.944 --> 00:05:06.927
The Colorado Center, which has a vast amount of experience with avalanches, also recommends you keep an eye

62
00:05:06.987 --> 00:05:10.288
out for patterns in the snow that show how it has been laid down.

63
00:05:11.309 --> 00:05:17.391
Knowing how to identify snow-covered areas that pose an avalanche risk, however, takes years of experience.

64
00:05:18.392 --> 00:05:23.334
A warning sign that is easier to see is bent trees that show where previous avalanches have taken

65
00:05:23.374 --> 00:05:28.676
place. Also, snow that makes a cracking or hollow sound could be unstable.

66
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.884
You should also watch for rocky outcrops or large trees that could prevent runaway slides.

67
00:05:36.104 --> 00:05:39.667
Staying close to these will show you safer routes around dangerous areas.

68
00:05:40.847 --> 00:05:45.851
If, despite these efforts, you are still caught in an avalanche, try first to get to the side

69
00:05:45.871 --> 00:05:49.293
of the snowslide, where the force of the moving snow is weakest.

70
00:05:50.114 --> 00:05:53.976
Grab onto any heavy object within reach, like a tree or large rock.

71
00:05:55.057 --> 00:05:59.460
If that's not possible, "swim" with the avalanche and try to stay on top of the snow.

72
00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:07.382
Although there are many tragic stories like Michel Trudeau's, there are also plenty of examples of people who

73
00:06:07.442 --> 00:06:11.063
manage to survive being buried beneath more than a meter of snow.

74
00:06:12.143 --> 00:06:17.845
Rescuers found one young woman buried by an avalanche in the mountains of Utah for almost two hours.

75
00:06:19.165 --> 00:06:25.927
Although she couldn't remember too many details, experts suspect that, before the avalanche came to a rest, she

76
00:06:25.987 --> 00:06:31.281
moved her head and shoulders as much as possible, creating a small pocket beneath the snow with enough

77
00:06:31.321 --> 00:06:32.341
air to keep her alive.

78
00:06:33.582 --> 00:06:39.404
The word "avalanche" comes from a word in a French dialect, avalance, which means simply "descent."

79
00:06:40.365 --> 00:06:43.826
But the image behind this word is one of a great unstoppable force.

80
00:06:44.646 --> 00:06:50.889
It has inspired the name of a professional hockey team, the Colorado Avalanche, and even a sport-utility vehicle,

81
00:06:51.249 --> 00:06:52.410
the Chevrolet Avalanche.

82
00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.712
While the image of tons of rolling snow thundering down a mountainside might be a source of awe

83
00:06:57.732 --> 00:07:02.696
and wonder, an avalanche remains one of the most dangerous forces that nature can unleash.
